<aside class="not-covered" aria-labelledby="not-covered-title">
	<h3 id="not-covered-title">What it does not protect against</h3>
	<p class="intro">
		The role's grants are the wall; the lease, the parser and the caps are fences on the
		laptop. Some things are outside all of them:
	</p>
	<ul>
		<li>
			<strong>The agent's other tools.</strong> Daypass controls the database tools only. What the
			model does with what it learned, in files, shells or other MCP servers, is outside its scope.
		</li>
		<li>
			<strong>The agent and your files.</strong> <code>config.json</code>, <code>lease.json</code>
			and <code>audit.jsonl</code> are protected by OS user permissions only (<code>0600</code>,
			owned by you). An agent you also allow to run shell commands as you can read a URL target's
			password, open a lease itself or edit the lease. The plugin's hook blocks the common forms;
			it is a fence, not a control.
		</li>
		<li>
			<strong>Columns you did not list.</strong> The hidden list is yours. <code>daypass hidden
			suggest</code> flags names that look sensitive; it does not read values. A phone number
			stored in <code>metadata</code> is not found by anyone, and a column added to a table
			without hidden columns is readable at once.
		</li>
		<li>
			<strong>Many cheap queries can still load the database.</strong> There is no rate limit.
			The 5-connection cap and the 10 s timeout bound each query, not the stream of them.
		</li>
		<li>
			<strong>SQL text in the audit file can hold PII.</strong> <code>WHERE phone = '+4915…'</code>
			is logged as written. The file is <code>0600</code> on the developer's laptop and is not
			sent anywhere; treat it as sensitive.
		</li>
	</ul>
	<p class="more">
		Postgres only, no MySQL. The full list, and which layer stops each threat: <a href="/docs/threat-model/">the threat model</a>.
	</p>
</aside>

